  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHot Temperatures Wednesday: Shortwave energy will move through\nthe northern plains during the day. This will begin to suppress\nthe ridge south. However before it completely does, one final \nday of extreme heat may occur. A frontal boundary will begin to \nmove into the area from the north during the afternoon. Ahead of\nthis front, winds will begin turning more southwesterly and \nthen westerly. Similar setups to this in the past have allowed\nfor hot temps and increased dew points (moisture pooling). \nWhile clouds and precip chances do make things uncertain (more \non that below), confidence is increasing that temps may be able\nto make it to the upper 90s to low 100s early enough in the day\nto allow for heat indicies to again reach 100-107, perhaps even\nfurther east than today. This would likely require an \nextension/expansion of heat headlines in future updates.\n\nThunderstorm Chances Wednesday - Thursday: As mentioned above,\na frontal boundary will be moving south into the area during the\nafternoon and further into the area during the evening. While\nmean ML CAPES look to be around 2000j/kg, wind shear will\ngenerally be marginal (25-30kts). With the mean flow being\nparallel to the front, scattered storms may form along or \nperhaps just behind the front. Any storms that can become more \norganized will have the potential to produce damaging winds and \nlarge hail however widespread severe weather is not expected at \nthis time. \n\nCoverage of showers and storms will likely increase from north\nto south during the evening and overnight hours with high\nprecip chances, which is welcome due to the drought. However,\nmean PW values around 2.0inches and convective elements could\nallow for rainfall rates to be high enough for some localized\nflooding. Latest REFS guidance suggests that higher potential\nfor this would be along and north of the Highway 54 corridor\nwith localized pockets of 2-4 inches. Most locations do have a \n70-90% chance of seeing at least 0.50 inch of rain with the\nhighest amounts expected north of Interstate 44. Showers and \nthunderstorms could linger through the day on Thursday as the \narea remains in the right entrance region of an upper level jet \nstreak. Widespread clouds behind the front looks to keep temps \nin the 80s on Thursday which would be welcome from the previous \ndays extreme heat. \n\nLate Week/Weekend Rain Chances/Temps: Additional disturbances\nlook to move through the area on a daily basis into and through the\nweekend. This features a rather pessimistic precip chance \nforecast (20-50%) as we will have to pin down each daily disturbance.\nExpect additional pop changes/modifications with future \nupdates. NBM temp spreads have shrunk some which leads to higher\nconfidence that temps will remain closer to average.",
